{
  "dom_id": "step-q70566",
  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "Determine the interval on which the function $f(t)=\\sqrt{t}\\,i+\\frac{1}{t-4}\\,j-5{t}^{2}\\,k$ is differentiable.",
  "worked_solution": "The vector-valued function $f(t)$ is defined on the interval $[0,4)\\cup (4,\\infty ).$ Let's study the differentiability of its components on this interval one-by-one.\n\n- The derivative of  $\\sqrt{t}$ is\n\n$$\n{(\\sqrt{t})}^{′}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}.\n$$\n\n \nSince the derivative is not defined at $t=0,$ we conclude that  $\\sqrt{t}$ is not differentiable at this point.\n\n- The derivative of  $\\frac{1}{t-4}$ is\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{1}{t-4})}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{(t-4)^{2}}.\n$$\n\n \nSince the derivative is defined for all the points on the interval $[0,4)\\cup (4,\\infty ),$ we conclude that  $\\frac{1}{t-4}$ is differentiable on this interval.\n\n- The derivative of  $-5{t}^{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{(-5{t}^{2})}^{′}=-10t.\n$$\n\n \nSince the derivative is defined for all the points on the interval $[0,4)\\cup (4,\\infty ),$ we conclude that  $-5{t}^{2}$ is differentiable on this interval.\n\nTaking into account that all the components of $f(t)$ are differentiable for all the points on the interval $[0,4)\\cup (4,\\infty ),$ except the point $t=0,$ we conclude that $f(t)$  is differentiable on $(0,4)\\cup (4,\\infty ).$",
